/**
 * Computed signatures of the `transforms` module (C++ operators, modules/transforms).
 *
 * The pass `reinterp` is declared in stdlib/transforms.cml. The entry here takes a function and
 * gives a function whose parameter list and result type come from the argument, which a
 * declaration cannot write (the arbitrary-function-type gap): `jvp(f)` is
 * `(f: (float) => float) => (float, float) => (float, float)`, the type `JvpResolver` computes
 * from `jvpSignature(f)` in modules/transforms/jvp.cpp.
 *
 * `Jvp<F>` names the result type the resolver computes:
 *   - every ordinary parameter of `F` becomes two parameters, the value and its tangent, so
 *     `jvp(f)` for `f(x, y)` takes `(x, dx, y, dy)`;
 *   - the result is the pair `(value, tangent)`, the same shape in and out;
 *   - `F` keeps its `with` parameters: each is one port of the lifted function, of the declared
 *     type, and the tangent of a captured value is the zero of its type -- a value that reaches the
 *     body through a `with` parameter does not move in forward mode. So `jvp(f)` for `f` of `<m:
 *     Model> (x: float)` is a function of `<m: Model> (x, dx)`.
 *   - `F` must take at least one ordinary parameter, every ordinary parameter a `float` and a
 *     `float` result, because a tangent is a `float` direction.
 * The tangent of an input is the seed: `jvp(f)(2.0, 1.0)` asks for the derivative in the
 * direction 1, and `jvp(f)(2.0, 2.0)` for twice it. Nothing here picks the direction for the
 * caller, so a function of several parameters is read one direction per run (`jvp(f)(3.0, 1.0, 2.0,
 * 0.0)` is `df/dx` at (3, 2)).
 * It is not a type a program can write.
 *
 * For reference only: these declarations are not compiled. They are checked against the operators
 * the module registers, and docs/stdlib-reference.md is generated from them.
 */

/// The tangent space of `T` (design principles 9.9); `stdlib/types.cml` declares it, and it is
/// named again here because it is the whole vocabulary of what follows. A forward transform carries
/// `Lift<T> = (T, Tangent<T>)` (`stdlib/rulesets/jvp.cml`), so the cut `split` makes between "the
/// part computable now" and "the part computable later" is the cut between those two components --
/// not a position among the parameters (author, 15:25).
/// @example is_same::<Tangent<float>, float>()    // true
export type Tangent<T>


/// Forward mode: `jvp(f)` gives a function that takes the parameters of `f` followed by their
/// tangents and returns the pair `(value, tangent)`. `jvp(f)(2.0, 1.0)` is the value `f(2.0)`
/// together with the derivative of `f` at 2 in the direction 1.
/// @example let forward = jvp((x: float) => x * x)
export func jvp::<F>(f: F): Jvp<F> when is_function::<F>()
/// The step of `vjp` that separates a lifted function into the part computable now and the linear
/// residue (design 4.2, 4.4). Implemented for the straight-line tier (`modules/transforms/split.cpp`):
/// the halves are built from the graph, and what crosses the cut -- the residuals -- is read off the
/// graph too, so a body that needs a value it computed carries it instead of computing it again.
///
/// The cut, as the author rules it (15:25; revised 2026-11-01 and 2026-11-02 08:30): the front half
/// takes the ordinary parameters and answers `(result, lin)`; `lin` is the back half, closed over the
/// residuals -- the values it reads -- and takes the tangents and nothing else.
///
///     split(f) = (fwd)  where  fwd(...values) = (result, lin)
///     lin      = the back half, its residuals captured rather than passed
///
/// The residuals are therefore **not a position in any type**: they are `lin`'s closure tuple, whose
/// layout the run-time kernel reads off the back half's graph (the capture ports the graph declares),
/// one member per residual, in the order the graph names them. At a call site `lin` is an ordinary
/// function type, so no signature states a count it cannot know and no guard has to admit one
/// (`ResidualsOf<F>`, and the `_` position before it, are both gone). `lin` is linear in the tangent
/// it is given -- the condition the transpose step (M3) reads -- and both halves speak in `Tangent<T>`
/// at the top of this file.
/// @example let step = split
export func split(f: (v: float, d: float) => (float, float)): (v: float, d: float) => (float, (float, float) => float)

/// The transpose of a linear function: `transpose(f)` takes the cotangent of `f`'s result and
/// gives the cotangent of its parameter. The data flow runs backwards -- each primitive is replaced
/// by the rule the `rulesets.transpose` family files under it, and a value used k times receives k
/// cotangents that add. `f(x) = 2*x + 3*x` transposed at the cotangent `1.0` gives `5.0`.
///
/// The transposed function takes one value, the cotangent of `f`'s result, and answers one cotangent
/// per parameter. Both lists are `f`'s own: every parameter of `f` is a linear input, and the
/// cotangent it is answered for is the one the caller hands in.
///
/// A value the transposed body needs beside the cotangent is not passed to it: it is a residual the
/// function that is transposed was closed over, and the body reads it as a closure port. That is the
/// same rule for a value the body computed, a value it read from outside, and a `with` value it read
/// (`lin`'s residual tuple): a parameter with no tangent space is a value, and it is the one place
/// the transposed graph takes a port that is not a cotangent, because the call site still fills it.
///
/// **`stdlib/types.cml#Transpose` is the contract**: the build makes the declared type, and the build
/// that makes it is `modules/transforms/transpose.cpp#transposeSignature`. So `transpose(f)` is
/// called as `transpose(f)(1.0)` for a one-`float` function -- one argument, the cotangent.
/// @example let back = transpose((x: float) => 2.0 * x + 3.0 * x)
export func transpose::<F>(f: F): Transpose<F> when is_function::<F>()


/// Batching: `vmap(f)` gives the function that applies `f` to every row of a batch. A row is one
/// dimension less than the batch: for `f: (f32) => f32` it is `(f32[]) => f32[]`, and for
/// `f: (f32[3]) => f32` it is `(f32[?, 3]) => f32[]` -- `Lift<T> = RowsOf<T>`, the value with one
/// dimension of unknown length in front of it (`stdlib/types.cml`). The arithmetic of every
/// elementwise primitive is that primitive (`bx + bw` is `x + w` element by element), which is why
/// `vmap(f)(xs)` and the row-by-row `map` answer the same bits.
///
/// The rules come from `stdlib/rulesets/vmap.cml`: the transform asks that family, by the operator's
/// key, for every primitive of `f`, and calls the function the rule names with the batches. An
/// operator with no rule is refused by name at the primitive, never batched by a guess; a call, a
/// branch or a loop in the body is refused the same way, because the elementwise tier batches
/// straight-line bodies.
/// @example let batched = vmap((x: f32) => x * 2.0)
export func vmap::<F>(f: F): Vmap<F> when is_function::<F>()

/// Memory for recomputation: `remat(f)` gives a function whose graph is `f`'s graph with the
/// property "a call of it is recomputed on the backward pass instead of kept" (the author's ruling
/// of 2026-11-01). The property is the graph's, not the call site's and not a tag's: the call
/// answers with a new graph whose body is the body it was given, so a call through it is a call of
/// the same function and no value changes -- what changes is what a step that cuts the graph in two
/// has to keep across the cut. `split` reads the property: the values of a marked graph that cannot
/// be recomputed from the parameters are not residuals, and the backward half computes them again
/// (`modules/transforms/split.cpp`). JAX writes the same transform as `jax.checkpoint`, whose other
/// name is `jax.remat`; in this tree "checkpoint" is the persistence module `ckpt`, so the transform
/// takes the other word.
/// @example let remembered = remat((x: int) => x + 1)
///
/// The type is the argument's own: a function in, the same function out, which is the whole of what
/// the name says. `modules/transforms/remat_operator.cpp` answers `(f: F) => F` and builds the graph
/// the value stands on; the shapes a call site can write today are the ones `jvp`'s and `split`'s
/// declarations show, because the result of a call is the exit type its resolver gives.
export func remat::<F>(f: F): F when is_function::<F>()

/// `FirstWithAsParam<F>`, written out as a type function so that it is one law: `F`'s remaining
/// `with` parameters keep their places, the first one becomes the first ordinary parameter, and the
/// rest of the ordinary list and the result are `F`'s own.
///
/// The first ordinary position is the whole point of the order: `jvp` and `vjp` differentiate the
/// ordinary parameters, and a pullback answers one cotangent per parameter in the order the
/// parameters are written, so a weight moved to the front is the cotangent a library `grad` reads
/// first. Moving it to the end would answer the same numbers for a two-parameter call and the wrong
/// member for a gradient.
/// @example-fragment `weighted` is a function the program declares, because the example needs a `with` parameter
/// @example is_same::<FirstWithAsParam<<x: float> (y: float) => float>, (x: float, y: float) => float>()    // true
export type FirstWithAsParam<F> = match type WithOf<F> { case (W, ...Rest) => <...Rest> (W, ...ParamsOf<F>) => ResultOf<F> }

/// The signature reinterpretation: `f` with its first `with` parameter moved onto the front of the
/// ordinary list, and its body untouched. The design's name for this kind of step is
/// `reinterp<first_with_as_param>(f)` (design principles 9.9: `reinterp<H>(f)` gives every primitive
/// the meaning `H` gives it); in this tree the kind is the operator's own name, the way `jvp(f)` is
/// `reinterp<jvp>(f)`, because a pass cannot answer a function value.
///
/// The body is not read, rewritten or re-derived -- the one thing that differs is which list a port
/// is in -- and a `with` value the body reads is read through a port like any other value, so a
/// reclassification of the port is the whole of the transform
/// (`modules/transforms/first_with_as_param_operator.cpp`). The transform is what makes a weight
/// differentiable by the library's composition: a `with` parameter has no place to take a direction
/// in from, and an ordinary one has.
///
/// Three shapes have no first `with` parameter to move, and each is refused where it is written: a
/// function with a `with` pack (the pack stands for however many arguments a call gives past the
/// fixed ones, so it has no first member), a `var` `with` parameter (a variable is read and written
/// by name, not handed in by a call), and a `constas` one (part of the signature's identity rather
/// than a value).
/// @example-fragment `weighted` is a function the program declares, because the example needs a `with` parameter
/// @example let reinterpreted = first_with_as_param(weighted)
export func first_with_as_param::<F>(f: F): FirstWithAsParam<F> when is_function::<F>()
